  ],
  "text": "Every single day on Earth is a tiny bit longer than the one before it. A watch says a day is exactly twenty four hours. Always the same. But the Moon's pull is quietly acting like a brake on that spin. Slow a spin down, and the energy has to go somewhere. The Moon drifts outward. We know this from ancient coral. Its growth rings work like a fossil clock. Run it forward instead, and every generation ticks just a little slower than the last. Run the clock back far enough, and a dinosaur's day was only twenty three hours. So the watch on your wrist is quietly falling behind the sky. One blink at a time. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
